Swedish skipper Pair Neiman was dismasted late on November 22 in the Golden Globe race and is now jury rigging toward Port. This is Offshore North, Episode 14. Every four years, a small number of sailors set out alone to try to become the first Canadian to finish the Vendée Globe. Out in the fleet this week, a dismissing in the Indian Ocean has ended one Golden Globe campaign, while a major race series locks in a new long-term home, and a large trimoran begins the long-toe home after a capsize. Sixteen solo skippers started the 2026-2014.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:33.740
2007 Golden Globe Race on September 6 from Lesabla de Lone, in France.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:39.340
The event runs in a retro format limited to 32 to 36-foot production boats designed

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:43.220
before 1988 and sailed without modern electronics or outside assistance.

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:49.300
Swedish skipper Pair Neiman, holding 6-place aboard the Russeller 36-Lazy Otter, losses

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:54.540
masked in heavy weather late on November 22, roughly 1,000 nautical miles southeast of Cape

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:54.820
Town.

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:59.760
winds were reported from the west at 35 knots, shifting south-west with gusts to

