00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:36.200
The 105-foot Altum Trimeran Bonk Popular 11, which capsized in the mid-Atlantic during

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:41.500
the route to RUM has been righted in the Azores and is now under tow for the 1,300 nautical

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:46.600
mile return to its base in Lorient. The recovery removes an immediate safety hazard from the race

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:51.680
area and returns the boat to its short team for assessment. A major capsize and subsequent writing

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:56.460
in the ultim class highlights how quickly a single structural or handling failure can end a campaign

00:03:56.460 --> 00:04:02.260
even on the largest and most heavily supported boats. On the Canadian boat as of November 23rd,

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:09.740
2026, the team's YB tracker shows Amira 4 at 50.47 degrees north, 1.28 degrees west.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:14.820
On 21 November, Canada Ocean Racing posted that Scott Schaulier and Alan Roberts will

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:20.160
sail the foiling IMOC, double-handed in the RORC transatlantic race starting January

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:23.260
10, 2027 from Lanzarote to Antigua.

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:28.940
The post notes the boat's potential for significantly higher downwind speeds than their previous IMOCA,

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:33.020
